# One person, three author strings.
#
# The history carries `thedamon-wizard <amon.koike@daemons.jp>` (a typo for the GitHub
# handle), `a-koike <amon06251994@gmail.com>` and `Amon Koike <amon.koike@daemons.jp>`.  Exact
# counts are deliberately not written here: they move with every commit, and a comment that
# goes stale on the next push is worse than one that states only the shape.  Run
# `git log --format='%an <%ae>' --no-use-mailmap | sort | uniq -c` for the current split.
# This submission states a sole author, so a reviewer running
# `git shortlog -sne` on the public repository would otherwise see three contributors and have
# to guess whether that contradicts the claim.
#
# Mapping is by commit email, which collapses both daemons.jp name variants and the gmail
# address without rewriting a single commit.  History is evidence; a canonical-name file is the
# supported way to correct how it reads.
Amon Koike <amon.koike@daemons.jp> <amon.koike@daemons.jp>
Amon Koike <amon.koike@daemons.jp> <amon06251994@gmail.com>
